"""Original distributed-systems and resilience lessons, with explicit failure boundaries."""
from teaching.foundations import put, LESSONS
put(14, '''Distribution removes the ability to observe one coherent machine state. A request may succeed on one node while another is unreachable, and a caller may not know whether a timed-out write committed. Designing a distributed system means specifying allowed histories under those uncertainties. Begin with one authoritative owner and explicit operations; add replication or coordination to meet a concrete need. Additional nodes create failure combinations as well as capacity, so correctness must be a protocol rather than a diagram of reachable boxes. || One logical operation / Several independently failing participants / Caller has incomplete information / Protocol defines allowed outcomes

A partial failure affects some participants or paths while others keep operating. The payment provider can charge successfully while the order service loses the reply. Rolling back the local order cannot reverse that external event. Track an uncertain state with a stable provider reference and reconcile it. Design compensations only for effects that can actually be compensated, and distinguish an operation not attempted from one attempted with unknown outcome. Treating every exception as a clean failure creates duplicate business effects. || Provider accepts payment / Order service loses response / Payment state is uncertain locally / Reconciliation establishes actual outcome

A slow network and a failed node can look the same to a participant for a while. Timeouts are practical failure suspicions, not perfect failure detectors. A retry can reach another node after the original is merely delayed. Use deadlines and bounded retries to protect resources, but let the protocol prevent conflicting owners or duplicate effects. Availability decisions must state when the system prefers rejecting work to risking an invariant, rather than assuming every missing heartbeat proves the old owner stopped. || Heartbeat delayed / Observer suspects failure / Old participant may still run / Protocol fences conflicting authority

A fault model states what kinds of failure a protocol tolerates: crash-stop, crash-recovery, message loss, partitions, or arbitrary Byzantine behavior. A majority algorithm designed for non-malicious crash failures does not automatically tolerate participants lying about their logs. Durability also depends on whether persistent state survives restart and on failure independence. Name these assumptions before counting nodes. Three replicas on one failing device do not provide the same protection as independent durable replicas. || Choose crash or Byzantine model / State storage-survival assumptions / Define network uncertainty / Evaluate protocol only within model

Wall clocks support timestamps and scheduling but can drift, jump, or be adjusted. A timestamp earlier than another does not necessarily establish causality across machines. Duration measurement should use an appropriate monotonic clock where available. If a reservation expires based on time, define the authoritative clock and tolerated uncertainty. Ordering requirements often need a logical sequence or consensus log rather than trusting client-generated timestamps. Record both source and observed time when their distinction matters for debugging. || Node A clock says 10:01 / Node B clock says 10:00 / Messages can still flow A to B / Clock order does not establish cause

Physical clocks estimate real time; logical clocks represent ordering constraints. A logical counter can advance without representing seconds, while a physical timestamp can compare unrelated events without proving causal order. Use physical time for user-facing dates and appropriate deadline policies, and logical order for protocol relationships where needed. Combining them requires a precise model. Do not subtract two arbitrary hosts' timestamps and call the result network latency without accounting for clock synchronization error. || Physical clock estimates time / Logical clock tracks ordering / Different questions need different clocks / Synchronization error bounds interpretation

Lamport clocks advance locally and on receive using max(local,received)+1. If event A happens before B through program or message order, its Lamport value is smaller; the converse need not hold. Vector clocks track per-participant causal progress and can identify concurrent versions under their assumptions, at metadata cost. An example with vectors [1,0] and [0,1] is concurrent. Neither clock by itself resolves a conflict or establishes a global real-time commit order. || A event has vector [1,0] / B independent event has [0,1] / Neither vector dominates / Merge policy must handle concurrency

A consistency model defines legal observation histories. Ask what a user may read after a write, after switching replicas, and after seeing another user's action. A post body and a feed count can have different needs. Stronger constraints can require coordination or reduce allowed serving behavior during partitions. “Strong” is ambiguous without naming the model, and “eventual” alone omits conflict and freshness details. Write concrete histories and identify which observations are acceptable before selecting a database setting. || User writes post P / Another region reads / Possible absence or presence / Chosen consistency contract defines legality

Strong consistency is often used informally for linearizable behavior, but must be defined in context. Eventual convergence says replicas should agree after updates cease and communication and repair assumptions hold; it does not promise a fixed staleness bound or excuse lost acknowledged writes. A display feed may accept temporary delay while the author's profile reads its own post promptly. Specify per-operation behavior and conflict resolution rather than labeling the whole application with one vague adjective. || Write arrives in region A / Region B initially lacks it / Replication and repair proceed / Convergence depends on stated assumptions

Linearizability makes completed operations appear to take effect atomically at points between invocation and response, respecting real-time precedence. If a write of 2 completes before a read starts, the read cannot return the overwritten 1 in a linearizable register. Concurrent operations can admit more than one valid ordering. Implementing the property requires a suitable protocol, not only majority counts or wall-clock timestamps. A timed-out operation may still have taken effect because it lacks a completed response. || Write 2 completes / Later read begins / Read must respect completed write / Overlapping operations permit legal order choices

Serializability concerns equivalence of transactions to a serial execution; linearizability concerns real-time atomic behavior of operations. A serial order need not follow observed wall-clock order unless a stronger contract requires it. Strict serializability combines serial transaction behavior with real-time precedence. These distinctions matter when a system mixes transactions, replica reads, and external messages. A serializable database transaction does not automatically make a payment provider call part of one real-time atomic operation. || Transaction history has serial equivalent / Real-time precedence is separate / Strict serializability adds that constraint / External effects remain outside local transaction

Sequential consistency preserves each participant's program order in some shared ordering without necessarily honoring real-time order across participants. Causal consistency preserves cause-and-effect dependencies while allowing unrelated concurrent events to be observed in different orders. If a reply depends on seeing a post, exposing the reply before its parent violates that causal relationship. Metadata and dependency tracking can enforce it, but an ordinary replication delay setting alone does not establish a causal contract. || User sees post P / User writes reply R to P / Another replica shows R / It must satisfy required causal dependency on P

Read-your-writes keeps a session from losing visibility of its own completed updates; monotonic reads prevent that session from moving backward in observed progress. They can be implemented through routing affinity, replication-position tracking, or suitable protocols. They are session guarantees, not necessarily global linearizability. A user switching devices needs a policy for carrying session progress. Sticky routing alone can fail after an instance change, so define how the guarantee survives failover or when a read must wait. || Session observes version 10 / Next request routes elsewhere / New owner must reach required progress / Do not return earlier version 9

CAP's impossibility applies to a defined distributed read/write model under partitions: its formal atomic consistency and availability cannot both be guaranteed for every allowed execution. It is not a menu where a system permanently picks any two attractive qualities. When replicas cannot communicate, an application may reject some operations to protect invariants or serve bounded weaker behavior for others. State the affected operation, partition assumption, and required property. Normal nonpartitioned latency trade-offs require additional reasoning. || Replicas separated by partition / One side accepts a write / Other side cannot know that write / Serving all reads conflicts with atomic visibility

PACELC highlights two questions: during a partition, availability versus consistency; otherwise, latency versus consistency under the considered design. It is a useful classification lens, not a universal theorem replacing a protocol specification. Waiting for a geographically distant acknowledgment can increase write latency even when the network is healthy. Different operations can choose different paths. Quantify propagation and coordination costs with explicit assumptions, and avoid assigning a permanent one-letter label to a product without checking its configuration. || Partition behavior decision / Normal-operation coordination decision / Geographic acknowledgment adds delay / Operation-specific contracts guide the choice

With fixed N replicas, R+W>N ensures a read set intersects a write set; W>N/2 ensures two write quorums intersect. These are set properties, not a complete proof of linearizability. Version ordering, concurrent writes, durable acknowledgments, membership, and read repair matter. Sloppy quorums or changing membership can invalidate naive assumptions. In N=3,R=2,W=2, a read sees at least one participant in a completed write set, but still needs a correct rule for interpreting returned versions. || Replicas: A, B, C / Write set: A and B / Read set: B and C / Intersection B is necessary evidence, not full protocol

Split brain means participants act as conflicting authorities. A partitioned old leader may keep running after another is elected. A failure detector cannot directly stop it. Use an ownership protocol and enforce epochs or fencing at the resource that accepts effects. Merely expiring a client-side lock variable is insufficient if an old worker can still write to storage. Test paused workers and delayed messages, not only clean process termination. || Old leader pauses / New leader obtains higher epoch / Old leader resumes / Resource rejects stale epoch effects

Coordination establishes shared decisions such as ownership, configuration, or ordered metadata. Use it for invariants that genuinely need agreement, while avoiding a coordination round for independent operations that can safely commute. A coordinator must itself have a failure and recovery protocol; making one service named coordinator does not make it reliable. Bound how clients behave when it is unavailable, and distinguish cached configuration serving from issuing new exclusive ownership. || Need one authoritative decision / Consensus metadata records epoch / Clients act under that epoch / Resource enforces accepted authority

The fallacies of distributed computing warn against assuming reliable networks, zero latency, infinite bandwidth, static topology, free transport, homogeneous infrastructure, automatic security, or one administrator. Translate each into a concrete design check. A serialized payload crossing a team boundary needs version and authorization policy; a large fan-out needs latency and bandwidth accounting. These warnings are engineering prompts rather than formal guarantees. Use them to discover hidden assumptions in a diagram before an outage discovers them for you. || Locate remote boundary / Account for latency and loss / Verify security and version assumptions / Assign ownership across administrative boundaries

Safety excludes bad outcomes; liveness requires progress under stated conditions. “No seat is sold twice” is safety. “Every accepted valid booking eventually resolves” is a liveness-oriented obligation with environmental assumptions. A system that rejects all requests may protect the first but fail the second. Network models and failure thresholds can prevent unconditional progress guarantees. Write both properties, state what failures may block completion, and expose stuck work so operators can restore progress without violating safety. || Safety: no double allocation / Liveness: accepted booking resolves / Partition may require waiting / Recovery must preserve both obligations''')

put(15, '''A primary/replica design gives one participant authority for a class of writes and copies its state elsewhere. It simplifies conflict ownership but requires promotion and protection against stale primaries. State who may accept writes and which failures preserve acknowledged data. Read replicas may provide older observations. A topology with one primary and two replicas is not a consensus specification; acknowledgment, persistence, election, membership, and recovery rules determine its actual guarantee. || Primary owns writes / Replicas receive changes / Acknowledgment policy defines durability / Promotion must preserve authorized history

Leader/follower replication sends an ordered stream from one leader to followers. Followers apply progress and can serve suitable reads or participate in failover under the protocol. Async followers may lag, while sync acknowledgment can increase latency. A follower that has received bytes but not durably stored the required record may not provide the intended crash-recovery protection. Track receive, persist, commit, and apply positions separately when the implementation distinguishes them. || Leader appends record / Follower receives it / Required persistence is acknowledged / Apply progress controls readable state

Multi-leader replication allows writes at several authorities, often reducing local write latency or supporting disconnected regions. Concurrent writes can conflict and need resolution. Last-writer-wins based on unsynchronized timestamps can discard a meaningful update; deterministic convergence is not necessarily correct business merging. Use it for domains with justified conflict semantics, not an invariant that demands a single winner unless additional coordination exists. Observe replication loops, delete propagation, and recovery from prolonged disconnection. || Region A accepts edit / Region B accepts concurrent edit / Both versions propagate / Explicit merge or conflict resolution decides result

Leaderless designs let clients or coordinators contact several replicas and reconcile returned versions. Read repair, hinted handoff, anti-entropy, and quorum configuration are separate mechanisms with implementation-specific effects. A successful response from two of three nodes does not establish linearizability without correct version and membership rules. Model concurrent writes and stale replica recovery. Choose this family when its availability, conflict, and operational contract fits the data; do not describe it as coordination-free for every possible operation. || Coordinator contacts replicas / Responses carry versions / Reconcile under documented rules / Background repair restores appropriate copies

Synchronous replication waits for specified acknowledgments before completing a write; asynchronous replication allows acknowledgment earlier. “Synchronous” must name whether an acknowledgment means received, durably stored, or applied. With async copying, loss of the only up-to-date owner can lose recent acknowledged writes. Waiting for independent durable replicas strengthens protection within the fault model but increases latency and may block during failure. Configuration is part of the application contract and must be tested during promotion. || Write reaches leader / Required replica acknowledgment defined / Caller receives success / Failure loss depends on that boundary

A quorum is a permitted decision set under a protocol. Majority intersection can preserve evidence across elections or writes when persistence, voting, and membership rules hold. In a three-node fixed group, any two majorities share at least one node. That node must also obey rules about what it can acknowledge and later support. Quorum arithmetic without durable state or correct election eligibility is insufficient; a quorum is a component of a protocol, not a universal consistency switch. || Majority A+B / Majority B+C / Node B intersects / Persistent voting and log rules preserve evidence

Consensus makes nonfaulty participants agree on a decision sequence under specified assumptions. A replicated state machine applies the same ordered commands deterministically to maintain equivalent state. Nondeterministic choices such as current time or random IDs must be supplied or reconciled consistently rather than recomputed differently on each replica. Consensus does not automatically make external HTTP effects atomic with state-machine application. Keep those effects explicitly identified and deduplicated outside the replicated decision boundary. || Consensus orders command C / Replicas apply C deterministically / States evolve equivalently / External effects need separate coordination

Raft separates leader election, log replication, and safety. Terms identify election generations; a candidate needs a majority of votes and appropriate log freshness. Followers persist the protocol's required state. The leader replicates entries and advances commitment using Raft's rules, including the restriction on directly committing by majority counting to entries from its current term. This protects prior history through the protocol, not a generic “two acknowledgments always suffice” rule. Reads need a justified freshness mechanism too. || Election term advances / Majority selects eligible leader / Leader replicates ordered entries / Current-term commit rule protects history

Paxos uses numbered proposals and intersecting majorities to ensure that once a value is chosen, later successful proposals preserve it under the protocol. Prepare and accept phases gather and carry forward required prior evidence. Multi-Paxos extends the approach for ordered decisions, often optimizing around a stable leader. This is a conceptual introduction, not executable consensus code. Production correctness depends on persistence, membership, retries, and liveness details; use a reviewed implementation rather than assembling a lock from the analogy. || Higher proposal prepares / Majority reports accepted evidence / Proposer preserves required value / Majority acceptance chooses under protocol

A replicated log needs both an ordering rule and a durability rule. An entry present on one node is not necessarily committed, and an applied local state is not necessarily safe to expose as globally authoritative. Recovery must distinguish committed prefixes from conflicting uncommitted suffixes. Do not return success because bytes reached a follower unless the protocol says that event establishes the promised outcome. Observe commit and apply progress, and make snapshot installation preserve the same history assumptions. || Log entry appended / Entry replicated but not yet committed / Protocol establishes committed prefix / State machine applies committed history

Membership changes alter which groups can make decisions. Replacing an old majority with a disjoint new majority in one step can allow conflicting histories. Protocols such as Raft use a coordinated transition, including overlapping quorum requirements in joint consensus. Learners may catch up before becoming voting members. A cluster-size setting is not merely an operations toggle: apply the supported membership protocol, preserve persistent identities, and test interruption during reconfiguration. || Old voter configuration / Controlled overlapping transition / New members catch up / New configuration becomes authoritative

A fencing token is a monotonically ordered ownership epoch checked by the resource receiving an effect. Worker A holding token 7 can pause past its lease while B obtains token 8. When A resumes, the protected store must reject token 7. A token generated but never enforced at the side-effect boundary does not fence anything. The receiving resource must atomically compare and apply, and the design must account for operations already accepted before the newer owner appeared. || Worker A holds epoch 7 / Worker B obtains epoch 8 / Store records highest accepted epoch / Later epoch 7 effect is rejected

Failure detection observes missing or delayed evidence and produces suspicion under timing assumptions. Short detection intervals can reduce failover delay but increase false suspicions during pauses or network congestion. The safety protocol must remain correct when suspicion is wrong. Separate detector tuning from authority transfer and resource fencing. A successful heartbeat also does not prove that the node can meet every business operation's deadline, so readiness and capacity signals remain useful alongside protocol heartbeats. || Heartbeat expected / Delay crosses suspicion threshold / Election or failover may start / Safety survives an incorrect suspicion

Strongly consistent metadata services can coordinate configuration, ownership epochs, and small control-plane decisions. They are usually unsuitable as the high-volume store for every application payload. Cache safe configuration for serving where appropriate, and specify behavior when new ownership cannot be issued. A metadata outage can freeze deployments or leases while existing data paths continue; that distinction should appear in the failure model. Avoid making every request synchronously depend on a control plane unless its cost is justified. || Small metadata decision / Consensus preserves authoritative epoch / Data plane uses cached safe state / New decisions pause when authority unavailable

A lock grants a form of exclusive permission; a lease grants it for a time under clock and expiry assumptions. A paused owner may act after its lease ends, so resource-side fencing is often needed for correctness-critical effects. Releasing must verify owner identity rather than blindly delete another owner's lock. Lock acquisition and protected work are separate events. Prefer a single authoritative data transaction for a local invariant when it avoids an unnecessary distributed locking protocol. || Acquire lease with owner identity / Perform bounded protected work / Lease expires or owner pauses / Resource rejects stale owner through fencing

CRDTs arrange updates and merges so replicas converge under stated algebraic and delivery assumptions. A grow-only set can merge by union; an observed-remove set needs metadata to distinguish observed additions from concurrent ones. Convergence does not enforce arbitrary invariants such as “stock never below zero.” Choose a type whose semantics match the domain, preserve required metadata, and bound tombstone growth or compaction safely. An explicit conflict review may be better for business edits without a natural automatic merge. || Replica A adds item X / Replica B adds item Y / Merge by union yields X,Y / Domain invariants still require separate reasoning

Regional replication must define write authority, conflict behavior, acknowledgment geography, and disaster recovery. A nearby async acknowledgment has different data-loss exposure from a cross-region durable quorum. During a partition, some operations may wait or reject while stale-tolerant reads continue. Reconnection requires catch-up and reconciliation before arbitrary old owners resume. Test failback as well as failover: restoring an old primary without epoch and history control can reintroduce conflicting state after the visible outage ends. || Regions exchange history / Partition changes reachable authority / Chosen policy governs writes / Recovery and failback reconcile histories''')

put(16, '''Synchronous communication makes the caller wait for the result; asynchronous communication records or sends work whose completion is observed later. A receipt email can be deferred after an order commits, while payment acceptance may require a different response contract. Asynchrony moves waiting and failure handling into durable state and delivery protocols. A queue can absorb a short burst, but sustained arrival above completion still grows backlog. Define who owns accepted work after the initiating request ends. || Order transaction commits / Receipt intent becomes durable / Request can return / Worker later records delivery outcome

A queue commonly distributes work among consumers; a retained log preserves an ordered append history for replay; pub/sub distributes publications to subscribers under its specific retention and acknowledgment semantics. Products combine these features, so check the actual contract. A consumer that needs replay after an outage cannot rely on an ephemeral subscription alone. Choose retention, partitioning, and consumer progress rules from recovery requirements rather than assuming every broker is a durable event history. || Work queue assigns a job / Log retains ordered records / Pub/sub distributes to subscribers / Recovery depends on configured retention

Producers create messages, brokers accept or route them, and consumers interpret them. There are several distinct acknowledgments: producer acceptance, durable broker replication, consumer receipt, and completion of a business effect. Naming only “acknowledged” hides the boundary. Include stable message identity, schema version, ownership, and correlation metadata. A broker can be healthy while consumers repeatedly fail, so monitor end-to-end age and outcome rather than only broker uptime and enqueue rate. || Producer submits message / Broker acknowledges defined persistence / Consumer receives record / Business effect commits before its acknowledgment

Partitions provide ordered lanes in systems such as Kafka; consumer groups commonly distribute partitions among members, and offsets represent progress within a lane. Order is typically per partition, not across all partitions. Rebalancing can move ownership and cause work to repeat around an acknowledgment boundary. Commit offsets according to the effect contract and preserve stable keys for required ordering. One very hot partition can limit a group despite many idle consumers. || Key selects partition / Group assigns partition owner / Consumer processes offset 42 / Rebalance may replay uncommitted progress

Delivery terminology must identify where messages and effects are counted. A broker can deliver at least once while a transactional consumer produces one local database effect through deduplication. An external provider can still receive repeated calls. Describe the full path: source acceptance, retention, retry, effect commit, and progress acknowledgment. Strong guarantees at one stage do not automatically compose into an end-to-end guarantee across independent systems and failures. || Message accepted at source / Record retained or redelivered / Consumer effect commits / Progress acknowledgment defines replay behavior

At-most-once processing can acknowledge before an effect and lose work after a crash. It avoids certain duplicates at the cost of missing effects. It may suit disposable telemetry under a deliberate loss budget, but not an order that has been promised durable processing. The term does not imply “always one”; zero is allowed. Specify whether the guarantee concerns delivery attempts, processing starts, or committed results because different boundaries can produce different counts. || Consumer receives record / Progress acknowledged first / Process crashes before effect / Record is not retried: zero effects

At-least-once processing retries until acknowledged under retention and recovery assumptions, so duplicates are possible. Commit a durable effect before acknowledging and make repetition safe. A crash after commit but before acknowledgment replays the record. A uniqueness constraint on event identity within the same local transaction as the effect can prevent duplicate local changes. If the transaction marks processed before making an unprotected remote call, it can lose that call; the effect boundary matters. || Receive event E / Commit effect and E identity / Crash before acknowledgment / Replay E detects completed local effect

Exactly-once claims require a precise boundary. A transactional stream processor may atomically couple consumed progress and produced records within its supported system. That does not include an arbitrary email server or payment provider unless another protocol covers the side effect. Idempotency can give a single relevant effect for repeated attempts under a retention and identity contract; it does not mean execution started only once. Name failures and recovery assumptions instead of using the phrase as a universal broker property. || Consume input offset / Transaction couples progress and output / Broker-visible result has scoped guarantee / External HTTP effect remains outside boundary

Ordering can mean publication order, per-key log order, consumer start order, or effect-commit order. Parallel processing can preserve one while changing another. If account operations must apply sequentially, partition or coordinate by account and do not acknowledge later changes as if earlier ones completed. Poison messages require a policy: skipping can restore progress while breaking the business sequence. Use sequence numbers and explicit gap handling where the domain depends on ordered effects. || Account event sequence 10,11 / Parallel workers finish 11 first / Commit policy detects dependency / Handle gap before claiming ordered effects

A consumer acknowledgment permits the broker to advance or remove delivery state according to the protocol. If acknowledgment precedes a required effect, a crash can lose work; if it follows, a crash can repeat work. There is no magical placement that atomically covers an unrelated external system. Use local transactions, inbox/outbox records, provider operation keys, and reconciliation as appropriate. Keep acknowledgment deadlines compatible with processing time and lease extension rules so healthy long jobs are not repeatedly redelivered. || Effect starts / Effect commits / Consumer acknowledges / Lost acknowledgment triggers safe replay

Retry transient failures with a bounded policy and delay; dead-letter persistent failures with context and a path for repair. A dead-letter queue is not a completed business outcome. Include original identity, attempt count, failure reason, and source version, then alert or reconcile unresolved important work. Replaying a repaired record must preserve deduplication and ordering rules. An unbounded retry loop can block a partition or overwhelm a permanently invalid downstream request. || Attempt fails transiently / Backoff and retry within policy / Persistent failure quarantined / Repair and replay preserve original identity

A poison message repeatedly fails because of malformed data, incompatible schema, or an invariant violation. Distinguish it from a temporary dependency failure so retries do not waste the same resources forever. Quarantine it with evidence and decide whether dependent messages must wait. A parser bug can make an entire valid historical schema look poisonous; inspect error distributions and replay samples before discarding records. Privacy rules apply to diagnostic payloads retained in quarantine. || Decode or validate record / Deterministic failure repeats / Quarantine with bounded evidence / Decide sequence and repair policy

An idempotent consumer records event identity and its relevant local effect atomically. INSERT processed(event_id) under uniqueness and the corresponding state update must share one transaction if they protect the same invariant. A duplicate then returns the already established outcome. Retain identity long enough for permitted replay and delayed delivery. This protects local effects only; calling an external provider still requires its operation key or reconciliation. Deduplicating only in volatile memory fails after restart. || Begin local transaction / Insert unique processed-event ID / Apply business state change / Commit both or neither

Event sourcing stores domain events as the authoritative history from which state is derived. It supports audit and reconstruction when event semantics remain interpretable, but adds schema evolution, correction, replay, and privacy obligations. A snapshot accelerates reconstruction and must identify its event position. Do not replay external side effects while merely rebuilding state. Append-only history is not automatically a legally sufficient ledger or an authorization model; domain and operational constraints still need design. || Append domain event / Fold events into current state / Snapshot records position / Replay rebuilds state without repeating effects

CQRS separates command and query responsibilities or models when their requirements differ. An order command model can enforce transitions while a read projection provides a convenient customer history. The projection may lag, so clients need a read-after-write policy. CQRS does not require event sourcing, independent databases, or microservices. Its additional models and synchronization are justified when they simplify real complexity, not when a straightforward CRUD application already satisfies its access patterns. || Command updates authoritative model / Change feeds read projection / Query serves convenient view / Freshness contract explains projection lag

A transactional outbox records state changes and publication intent in the same local transaction. A relay later publishes pending intents and marks progress. If it publishes then crashes before marking, it can publish again, so consumers still deduplicate. The pattern closes a local database-to-publication gap; it does not make arbitrary subscribers part of that transaction. Use stable event IDs, retryable relay progress, retention, and reconciliation for intents that remain unprocessed too long. || Transaction updates order / Same commit inserts outbox event / Relay publishes event / Crash before mark can duplicate publication

A saga coordinates local transactions and compensating actions across ownership boundaries. Inventory reservation, payment authorization, and shipment creation cannot be treated as one ordinary local transaction. On failure, compensation might release stock or request a refund, but it can fail and may not perfectly reverse the original action. Persist workflow state and compensation progress. Distinguish recoverable intermediate states from final success or failure, and reconcile with external systems after uncertain responses. || Reserve inventory / Authorize payment / Shipment creation fails / Compensate and track unresolved actions

Choreography lets participants react to events without one central workflow coordinator; orchestration stores and directs the workflow explicitly. Choreography can reduce centralized coupling but makes end-to-end progress harder to see. Orchestration clarifies ownership and retries while introducing a coordinator and workflow schema to operate. Neither eliminates participant failures. Choose based on workflow complexity, auditability, change ownership, and compensation needs, and maintain a correlation identity through every step. || Participant events drive next action / Orchestrator alternatively issues commands / Both need durable participant outcomes / Observe one correlated business workflow

A workflow engine persists execution progress and resumes work after failures under its programming model. It can manage timers, retries, and human waits, but activities calling external systems can still execute more than once around acknowledgment gaps. Understand deterministic replay, versioning, activity identity, and cancellation rules of the chosen implementation. An engine is a tool for durable orchestration, not proof that every external side effect or business invariant has become exactly once. || Workflow state persisted / Activity dispatched / Result may be uncertain after worker loss / Resume with stable activity identity

Durable jobs have a persistent identity, input reference, state, attempt history, and ownership protocol. A worker should record completion under the current attempt or fencing epoch so a stale worker cannot overwrite a newer result. Store large payloads outside the queue and validate input version before work. Retry does not mean unlimited recomputation: set budgets and surface terminal or review states. A process-local background task is not durable merely because the request returned 202. || Persist job and input version / Worker claims attempt epoch / Work produces versioned result / Conditional completion rejects stale attempt

Scheduling combines a durable due-time record with worker coordination. A scheduler claims due work using an atomic transition or lease and creates a stable run identity such as (schedule_id,occurrence). Clock changes, missed periods, overlapping runs, and failover need explicit policy. A lease can expire while the old worker runs, so protect effects with fencing or idempotency at their resource. Exactly one scheduler process is not a substitute for a recoverable occurrence protocol. || Due occurrence identified / Atomic claim assigns epoch / Worker executes stable run ID / Expiry and recovery preserve effect identity

Backpressure controls admission or producer progress when consumers cannot keep up; replay deliberately revisits retained input. Replaying at full historical speed can overload live dependencies and delay current work. Give replay its own rate and capacity budget, preserve source identity, and isolate side effects. Retention must cover the recovery horizon. Queue length alone is not enough: monitor oldest age, per-key blockage, and meaningful completion rates so a seemingly busy pipeline does not conceal stuck business outcomes. || Consumer capacity limited / Producer slows or queue stays bounded / Replay uses separate budget / Completion age remains observable

Reconciliation compares intended and observed outcomes after partial failure. A payment marked pending locally may already be charged at the provider; query by stable operation identity and repair the local state rather than blindly charging again. Reconciliation must itself be idempotent, authorized, and observable. Run it periodically and during incident recovery, tracking unresolved discrepancies with age and ownership. It is an essential protocol stage, not an admission that the normal path can ignore persistence or validation. || Local intent remains uncertain / Read provider outcome by identity / Compare with local invariant / Repair once and record evidence''')

put(17, '''A failure domain groups elements exposed to a common cause: a process, host, zone, dependency, or administrative action. Isolate critical functions so one overload or crash cannot consume every resource. Two workers sharing one disk and credential source may fail together even in different processes. Map correlations and test them. Isolation has a cost in duplicate capacity, network calls, and operating work, so apply it according to the harm and recovery objectives of each failure. || Process boundary / Shared host and disk / Zone and dependency correlation / Isolation targets an explicit failure cause

Availability concerns useful serving behavior; durability concerns retained committed state under stated failures. A database can remain durable while unavailable during recovery. A stateless application can return 200 while silently dropping writes. Specify and observe both properties independently. Backups, replication, timeouts, and failover affect different parts of the contract. Do not promise a “reliable service” without explaining eligible requests, required outcomes, data-loss tolerance, and the time window or failure model being evaluated. || Request success indicator / Committed-state retention test / Recovery duration objective / Separate evidence for separate promises

Redundancy adds spare components or copies, but their usefulness depends on independence, capacity, detection, and state correctness. If two instances each run at 70% and one fails, the other cannot absorb all traffic without overload. Plan reduced-capacity behavior or reserve appropriate headroom. Shared configuration mistakes can disable all replicas simultaneously. Redundancy should be paired with tested traffic movement and recovery, rather than counted as a number of boxes in an architecture picture. || Two instances at 70% / One instance fails / Remaining load exceeds capacity / Headroom or shedding preserves essential service

Active-active serves from multiple locations or owners; active-passive keeps a standby for takeover. The choice concerns a particular function: applications may be active-active while a database has one write leader. Active-passive simplifies some authority decisions but can reveal cold-start and drift problems during failover. Active-active adds routing, coordination, or conflict complexity. Define failure detection, promotion, fencing, capacity, and failback for either model; a standby that has never restored usable state is unproven protection. || Normal serving topology / Standby or concurrent active owner / Failure transfers responsibility / Test data authority and return path

Timeouts bound waiting and resource ownership. Use an overall request deadline plus sensible stage limits for connection, pool acquisition, and operations. A timeout should trigger cleanup or cancellation where possible, but does not prove a remote action failed. Choosing every timeout independently can let downstream work outlive the caller's useful window. Propagate remaining time and avoid beginning expensive work that cannot finish within it. Record timeout stage to distinguish queueing from execution failures. || Establish end-to-end deadline / Allocate remaining stage budgets / Cancel owned work on expiry / Reconcile uncertain remote effects

Retries can recover transient faults but spend capacity. Use bounded attempts, exponential backoff, jitter, and error classification, with a stable identity for effects. A permanent validation failure should not be retried unchanged. Respect downstream retry guidance where applicable, but do not let a large Retry-After exceed the business operation's lifecycle unnoticed. Retry policies belong to the end-to-end design; one safe retry layer is often better than many independently aggressive layers. || Classify failure / Check safe identity and remaining time / Wait randomized bounded backoff / Retry or surface explicit outcome

A retry storm amplifies an outage when many callers repeat work against a struggling dependency. Three attempts at four nested layers can produce 81 downstream attempts per original intent. Random delay reduces synchronization, but an attempt budget and admission control are also needed. Preserve goodput by rejecting excess work and suppressing redundant attempts. Monitor original business intents separately from attempts so apparent traffic growth during an outage is not mistaken for healthy user demand. || One intent enters four layers / Each permits three attempts / Potential downstream attempts: 81 / Limit retries across the whole path

A circuit breaker tracks dependency failures and temporarily rejects or redirects calls under a policy. Common states are closed, open, and half-open, with bounded probes to test recovery. It prevents futile waiting but does not repair the dependency or decide domain correctness. A fallback that returns a fabricated payment approval is unsafe. Make failure classification, probe concurrency, and recovery thresholds explicit, and avoid every instance probing simultaneously after the same interval. || Closed allows calls / Failure threshold opens breaker / Open rejects quickly / Half-open probes bounded recovery

Bulkheads partition resources so one class of work cannot consume everything. Separate pools for payment authorization and optional receipt delivery can keep checkout viable when email stalls. Pool sizing must include shared limits such as database connections and host memory; separate thread pools alone do not isolate every dependency. Over-isolation can waste spare capacity. Choose boundaries by failure harm and observe saturation and rejection in each partition, not only fleet-level averages. || Critical payment pool / Separate optional email pool / Email stalls and saturates / Payment retains its reserved capacity

Rate limiting protects a boundary from excessive traffic under a defined identity and burst policy. A token bucket permits bounded bursts while enforcing a refill rate; a queue-based shaper changes timing rather than simply rejecting. For multi-tenant services, combine per-tenant fairness with global capacity protection. A limiter depending on an unavailable remote store needs a deliberate fail-open, fail-closed, or local-budget policy. Each choice affects abuse risk and availability, so test the failure mode explicitly. || Identify caller and operation / Consume allowed token atomically / Reject or delay excess / Enforce global and tenant budgets

Load shedding rejects lower-value or excessive work before resources are exhausted. A service may stop recommendations while preserving order status and payment reconciliation. Decide priorities from business meaning, and avoid spending expensive computation only to reject at the end. Shedding should return a clear outcome with bounded retry behavior. It improves useful completion under overload, but cannot make an unsustainable workload sustainable indefinitely; scaling, workload reduction, or a changed objective may still be needed. || Detect bounded-capacity limit / Admit essential work / Reject optional excess early / Observe goodput and fairness

Graceful degradation preserves a useful subset of behavior under failure. Showing product descriptions without recommendations can be acceptable; displaying stale available inventory as permission to sell may not be. Define which data may be stale, which actions must pause, and how users see the limitation. Test degraded paths because rarely used fallbacks can fail when needed. Degradation is a product and correctness decision, not simply catching every exception and returning a default value. || Recommendation service fails / Catalog remains authoritative / Page omits optional recommendations / Purchase invariants remain enforced

A fallback substitutes an alternative behavior with an explicit contract. A cached help page can replace a failed content request; a default account balance cannot replace a failed ledger lookup. Fallbacks can create load elsewhere and can mask a prolonged incident. Bound their freshness and duration, signal degraded operation, and check security scope. A second provider is not transparent if its semantics, operation identities, or reconciliation process differ from the first. || Primary dependency fails / Check whether substitute is valid / Serve bounded alternative or reject / Record degraded result and freshness

Health checks should answer a useful local question and be bounded. Restarting a process because a shared provider is down can create a restart storm without repairing the provider. Readiness may stop new work while allowing in-flight operations to finish. Liveness should avoid external dependencies that can fail independently. Include startup and warmup behavior, and distinguish “process responds to probe” from “critical business operations meet their objectives.” Probe success is evidence, not a complete reliability guarantee. || Startup initializes required local state / Readiness admits suitable traffic / Liveness detects stuck process / Business SLIs observe actual outcomes

RTO is the recovery time objective; RPO is the acceptable data-loss exposure expressed in time. If an incident begins at 12:00, service restoration at 12:20 gives a twenty-minute interruption; restoring data only through 11:55 creates five minutes of exposure relative to the incident point. These are separate objectives and measurements. Set them per business function, and verify detection, decision, restoration, validation, and traffic return in drills rather than timing only the database copy operation. || Last recoverable point: 11:55 / Incident: 12:00 / Service restored: 12:20 / RPO exposure 5 min; recovery 20 min

A backup must be restorable, authorized, decryptable, consistent enough for the application, and retained separately from the failure it protects against. Test actual restoration and verify business invariants, not just checksum or job status. Replication can copy corruption or accidental deletion, so historical recovery serves a different purpose. Document keys, credentials, schema versions, and log dependencies needed during a disaster. A backup whose only decryption key was on the lost host is unusable protection. || Backup captured / Independent storage and keys retained / Restore into clean environment / Validate application and reconciliation state

Failover transfers service or authority to a replacement; failback returns responsibility after repair. Both require state freshness, fencing, routing, and capacity decisions. Promote only under the chosen durability contract and prevent the old owner from accepting conflicting writes. Rejoining an old primary may require rebuilding its state from the new history. DNS, connection pools, caches, and client retries can prolong the transition even after a database promotion succeeds. Test the complete user-visible sequence. || Detect and choose promotion / Fence old authority / Route and validate service / Rebuild old owner before safe failback

Multi-zone and multi-region deployments protect against different correlated failures. Cross-zone copies may survive a host or zone incident but not a regional control-plane or operator error. Cross-region replication adds latency, network charges, data placement concerns, and recovery complexity. Avoid claiming region independence when both rely on one global credential or configuration component. Select topology from explicit failure goals, and reserve enough surviving capacity for the degraded traffic and catch-up work during recovery. || Host failure / Zone failure / Regional dependency failure / Each requires a deliberately independent boundary

Chaos engineering tests hypotheses about behavior under controlled faults. Start with an explicit claim, bounded blast radius, observability, stop conditions, and recovery ownership. For example, pause one worker and verify that its expired lease cannot overwrite a newer result. Randomly killing processes without a hypothesis can create incidents without learning much. Prefer safe environments and progressively justified scope; interpret a passing drill as evidence for the tested scenario, not proof against all future failures. || State reliability hypothesis / Inject bounded fault / Observe invariant and recovery / Stop and repair when limits are exceeded

Reliability effort should follow user-visible objectives and error-budget evidence. If a small fraction of expensive optional calls causes most deadline failures, removing them from the critical path may help more than another region. Avoid maximizing redundancy without considering cost and complexity. An objective that ignores critical low-volume operations can hide serious harm behind a large denominator. Segment indicators where necessary and use the budget as a decision aid, not permission to dismiss particular users' failures. || Measure meaningful SLI / Compare budget consumption / Locate highest-impact failure mode / Choose justified reliability investment

Cascading failure occurs when one fault creates additional pressure and failures elsewhere: a slow provider holds connections, queues grow, callers retry, and formerly healthy dependencies become overloaded. Break the feedback with deadlines, bounded admission, retry budgets, bulkheads, and meaningful degradation. Capacity must remain available for recovery work. A circuit breaker alone can still trigger a stampede against a fallback. Trace the complete resource path and test correlated failures rather than assuming each component fails in isolation. || Provider slows / Connections and queues accumulate / Retries multiply offered work / Admission and isolation break the loop

Incident analysis reconstructs the timeline, contributing conditions, detection gaps, and recovery decisions without reducing the explanation to individual blame. “Human error” does not explain why one action could disable every replica without a safe recovery path. Link corrective work to observed mechanisms and assign verification evidence. Prefer changes that reduce recurrence or harm, such as scoped credentials, tested restores, and bounded retries, rather than adding alerts that do not create a useful response action. || Reconstruct evidence timeline / Identify contributing system conditions / Assign concrete corrective work / Verify the changed failure behavior''')

